.TH GIT-PR-RESTACK 1 "2026-06-13" "git-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-pr-restack \- rebase one GitHub pull request branch onto a base
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git pr-restack
.RI [ options ]
.I number|url|branch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git pr-restack
rebases one open pull request head branch onto a base branch and pushes with
.BR \-\-force\-with\-lease .
By default, the base is the pull request's current base branch.
.PP
When a new base is given and the push succeeds, the command updates the GitHub
pull request base.
.PP
The target and every GitHub query or edit are pinned to the repository derived
from the current checkout. Before rebasing, the command separately resolves one
fetch URL for the checkout/base repository and one uniquely mapped push
remote with one effective target matching the structured pull request head
repository. It requires that target's live head OID to equal the PR record.
Named base and fork branches are fetched as exact refs/heads sources through the
validated base-repository URL, without named-remote refspecs or tags, and are
converted to immutable commit OIDs before rebase. Internal commit handoffs use
an explicit
.B oid:
namespace, so hex-looking branch names remain branches. The pull request head is
fetched directly from the validated head URL and must yield the same OID before
checkout. The effective push URL is
pinned against further insteadOf and pushInsteadOf rewrites and uses an exact
lease. Missing heads, ambiguous remotes, multiple push URLs, rewrite mismatches,
and concurrent updates fail closed. After push, the command requires GitHub's
PR head OID to equal the rewritten local branch before editing the PR base.
Stack orchestration additionally binds the discovered pull request number, head,
head OID, base, head repository, cross-repository state, and canonical URL at
each rebase, push, and base-edit boundary.
.PP
After checkout and immediately before rebase and push, the command requires
HEAD, the fully qualified local head ref, worktree cleanliness, and the expected
OID to agree. The force-push source is the captured immutable OID, so checkout
hooks or concurrent local ref changes cannot inject another commit.
.PP
Credential-bearing HTTP URLs, query or fragment suffixes, unsafe git-config key
bytes, and invalid GitHub owner or repository components are rejected before
exact transport helpers run. Normal SSH usernames remain supported.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-base " " \fIbranch\fR
Rebase onto this branch and update the pull request base.
.TP
.BI \-\-fork " ref"
Replay only commits after
.I ref
using
.BR "git rebase \-\-onto" .
Use this when the previous base was squash-merged, so its commits are dropped
instead of re-applied and conflicting.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-push
Rebase locally without pushing or editing the pull request base.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print actions without changing anything.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Pull request branch was restacked.
.TP
.B 1
Restack failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or local environment error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-pr-stack (1),
.BR git-pr-land-stack (1)
